    title: "An unexpected second life for the town cinema",
    text: "A. When the Regal cinema in Darnham closed, the last person out switched off a large red sign above the entrance. The sign remained there for four years. People passing it often said they wanted the building to open again. Fewer agreed on what should happen inside. Some wanted the same programme of recent films. Others imagined rehearsal rooms, a weekly market or a space for evening classes. The council owned the building but had very little money to spend on its running costs. A proposal had to show more than affection for the old sign.\n\nB. A local group organised an open day to hear suggestions. Its first questionnaire asked whether visitors supported saving the Regal. Almost everyone answered yes. A teacher on the committee noticed that the answers gave them no way to choose between proposals. On the next form they asked a more demanding pair of questions: which activity would you use, and on which day could you actually come? Several people who wanted a monthly concert could not attend the Friday evenings suggested by the committee. That difference changed the planned timetable. Even the improved form could reach only people present on the open day. The committee left copies in two shops and asked a youth group to discuss the questions at its next meeting. Responses from the shops included people who had never visited the Regal as a cinema. A committee member wondered whether their views should carry less weight than those of former customers. The teacher pointed out that a new building programme had to attract future users as well as honour memories. Someone who had never liked films might nevertheless become a regular at an evening language class.\n\nC. The building itself imposed limits. The auditorium was excellent for performances but expensive to heat for a group of six language learners. A smaller room behind the screen could be heated separately and had an outside entrance. Turning it into a classroom required little change apart from better lighting. The foyer, by comparison, was bright and much admired. It was also the only route from the main entrance to the auditorium. Using it for a crowded market during a performance would have blocked the flow of visitors.\n\nD. The group's first budget counted ticket income and the cost of electricity but overlooked the time required to open and close the doors. One volunteer happily ran an evening event. The same person was less happy to cross town at seven the next morning to let a cleaner in. The revised budget included a paid caretaker for a limited number of hours. Some members felt that paying somebody undermined the spirit of volunteering. Others argued that a reliable opening time made the volunteers' own work possible. The group tried the arrangement for three months before discussing a permanent contract. The caretaker's list of duties began as three short lines and occupied a full page by the end of the trial. A room had to be checked after use, a fault reported while it was still easy to describe, and any change of key holder entered in the book. These were small tasks that passed unnoticed when performed well. If one was missed, a teacher might arrive with eight learners and find a locked door. Recording the work helped the committee decide which tasks needed a named person and which could safely be shared between volunteers on different days.\n\nE. As the first season progressed, the committee learned to distinguish empty seats from an unsuccessful event. A discussion group might use only ten seats and still meet its purpose perfectly. For a film shown under an agreement that charged the cinema for every screening, ten tickets might fail to cover the cost. At each monthly meeting the organiser therefore reported the purpose, attendance and direct cost of each activity. Figures for different events remained visible, but nobody pretended they meant the same thing in every case. The report was short enough to be read at an ordinary meeting. When somebody proposed a new activity, the committee asked which of the existing measures would tell them whether it had achieved its purpose. This question often led to a clearer proposal before any booking was accepted.\n\nF. At the end of the year the red sign lit up again. The most emotional part of the ceremony was not the sight of the sign but a short speech from the cleaner who had first suggested an earlier opening time. The group had initially invited only performers to speak. Including somebody whose work kept the building available reflected a wider change. The Regal could now be understood as a timetable, a set of working arrangements and a number of small rooms as well as a beautiful auditorium. It had kept its old name while finding several new uses.",
